cos u \\\\ v\\cos u & \\sin u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-2v\\sin u \\cdot \\sin u - 2\\cos u \\cdot v\\cos u \\\\\n&=-2v(\\sin^{2} u + \\cos^{2} u) \\\\\n&=-2v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2\n$$" 13194139534943 false] [17592186128773 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/62dec67282c5bc2707377cb976412a48ca3c41ffeed6aa74ef0d5b1ef8d6e380.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/72902f1a278bc1316ff58c54940b4d5e65fa04aa8e1711c8e0a1726f57cd3641.png) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$" 13194139534943 true] [17592186128795 1100 "Consider a small rectangle $\\Delta$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta{A}$. Find an approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534943 false] [17592186128795 1100 "Consider a small rectangle $\\Delta$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta{A}.$ Find an approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/4c41597b6c647ba16bff7b6675d1bbe9d3c66b8cbb8e9a13bbb6651da6cd284e.png)" 13194139534943 true] [17592186128795 1145 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\big(\\mathbf{T}(\\Delta)\\big) \\approx \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{Area}(\\Delta)}_{\\large \\delta{A}}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{3} & 3u{v}^{2} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={v}^{3}\\cdot 2v-3u{v}^{2}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,2)} & =(2{v}^{4}-6{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{4}-6(-1)^{2}(2)^{2} \\\\  & =32-24 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|8|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =8\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534943 true] [17592186128795 1145 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\big(\\mathbf{T}(\\Delta)\\big) \\approx \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right| \\cdot \\underbrace{\\textrm{Area}(\\Delta)}_{\\large \\delta{A}}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}v^{3} & 3uv^{2} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= v^{3} \\cdot 2v - 3uv^{2} \\cdot 2u \\\\\n&= 2v^{4} - 6u^{2}v^{2} \\\\\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) \\mid _{(-1, 2)} &= (2v^{4} - 6u^{2}v^{2}) \\mid _{u =-1v = 2} \\\\\n&= 2(2)^{4} - 6(-1)^{2}(2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 32 - 24 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nArea(T(Δ))\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx & 8\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot δA; = 8δA\n$$" 13194139534943 false] [17592186128796 1100 "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." 13194139534943 false] [17592186128796 1100 "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/0e620d5ca71a48c87a0d5942ffa8378964f6172bff7396c290024a5299531204.png)\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." 13194139534943 true] [17592186128796 1145 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  <    0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/e947cb962ec1c0405292be18c838205b7be95ab083e07925d797b87e9c5b8654.png)\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{v} & u{e}^{v} \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{v}\\cdot (-2v)-u{e}^{v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{v}({u}^{2}+v) & <0 \\\\ {u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u^2\n$$\n\ndefines a downward-opening parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/2b6128c5a1737e3ad1fd82f91f5ccd320421ad3472ae8152440034ecea10d364.png)" 13194139534943 true] [17592186128796 1145 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} < 0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}e^{v} & ue^{v} \\\\ 2u & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= e^{v} \\cdot (-2v) - ue^{v} \\cdot 2u \\\\\n&=-2e^{v}(u^{2} + v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2e^{v}(u^{2} + v) & < 0 \\\\ u^{2} + v & > 0 \\\\ v & > - u^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nv=-u^2\n$$\n\ndefines a downward-opening parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1999/Source/Images/c1f0d183af763ad356f1fbb644c7e42e.png)" 13194139534943 false] [17592186128941 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534943 false] [17592186128941 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/ce4692599c5c9fe69ed9247d65dee5d982e1b7a2cf33b9070b799c94c321bad4.png)" 13194139534943 true] [17592186128941 1145 "Given a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx=(u+v)^3\n$$\n\nand $y=(u-v)^2$.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= 3(u + v)^{2} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|= 3(u + v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 2(u - v) & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|=-2(u - v)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y))/(∂(u, v)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}3(u + v)^{2} & 3(u + v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u - v) & -2(u - v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3(u + v)^{2} \\cdot (-2(u - v)) - 3(u + v)^{2} \\cdot 2(u - v) \\\\\n&=-12(u + v)^{2}(u - v)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534943 false] [17592186128941 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/59265be9c026880542032006b0c4aec9f1c8eeb2cd448423d38c17f75e1393cd.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=(u+v)^3$ and $y=(u-v)^2.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3(u+v)^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3(u+v)^{2} & 3(u+v)^{2} \\\\ 2(u-v) & -2(u-v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2(u-v))-3(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 2(u-v) \\\\  & =-12(u+v)^{2}(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534943 true] [17592186129122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321380 13194139534943 true] [17592186129122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321401 13194139534943 true] [17592186129122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321408 13194139534943 true] [17592186129122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321492 13194139534943 true] [17592186129198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321387 13194139534943 true] [17592186129198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321394 13194139534943 true] [17592186129198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321415 13194139534943 true] [17592186129198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321422 13194139534943 true] [17592186129217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321429 13194139534943 true] [17592186129217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321464 13194139534943 true] [17592186129217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321471 13194139534943 true] [17592186129217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321478 13194139534943 true] [17592186129217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321485 13194139534943 true] [17592186129218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321436 13194139534943 true] [17592186129218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321443 13194139534943 true] [17592186129218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321450 13194139534943 true] [17592186129218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321457 13194139534943 true] [17592186129438 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534943 true] [17592186129438 1100 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - 2v^{2} \\\\ u - v\\end{bmatrix}$,\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" 13194139534943 false] [17592186129438 1100 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" 13194139534943 true] [17592186129438 1145 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -4v \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-1)-(-4v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2u+4v & <0 \\\\ 4v & <2u \\\\ v & <\\frac{1}{2}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{2}u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043620/assets/09f7f36ecc2197cc95e1878fa22ac22367978809a5241f09886a7eb2fab071f3.png)" 13194139534943 true] [17592186129443 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534943 true] [17592186129443 1100 "For the transformation $T$, defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}e^{u + v} \\\\ e^{u - v}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534943 false] [17592186129443 1100 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {e}^{u-v}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534943 true] [17592186129443 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)={e}^{u-v}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u-v} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-{e}^{u-v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ {e}^{u-v} & -{e}^{u-v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (-{e}^{u-v})-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot {e}^{u-v} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2u}-{e}^{2u} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,-5)}=-2{e}^{2(0)}=-2.\n$$" 13194139534943 true] [17592186129568 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534943 true] [17592186129568 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$, defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2u\\cos v \\\\ 3u\\sin v\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534943 false] [17592186129568 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v \\\\ 3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534943 true] [17592186129568 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$ and $y(u,v)=3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot 3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-(-2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =6u({cos}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534943 true] [17592186321380 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321381 13194139534943 true] [17592186321380 1089 "q-160381" 13194139534943 true] [17592186321380 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534943 true] [17592186321380 1100 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3u+2v \\\\ u+3v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534943 true] [17592186321380 1113 #uuid "51ad204a-d196-50d6-898f-5c163645b62a" 13194139534943 true] [17592186321380 1145 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3u+2v$ and $y(u,v)=u+3v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534943 true] [17592186321381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321382 13194139534943 true] [17592186321381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321383 13194139534943 true] [17592186321381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321384 13194139534943 true] [17592186321381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321385 13194139534943 true] [17592186321381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321386 13194139534943 true] [17592186321381 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534943 true] [